'''Philip Jacob Weiser''' (born May 10, 1968) is an American attorney and politician who has served as the [[Attorney General of Colorado|attorney general of Colorado]] since 2019. He is the Hatfield professor of law and telecommunications, executive director and founder of the Silicon Flatirons Center for Law, Technology, and Entrepreneurship, and dean emeritus at the [[University of Colorado Law School]].<ref name="faculty">[http://lawweb.colorado.edu/profiles/profile.jsp?id=62 Professor Philip J. Weiser faculty profile]. University of Colorado. Retrieved September 15, 2017.</ref> He previously served in the [[Presidency of Barack Obama|Obama]] and [[Presidency of Bill Clinton|Clinton administrations]] in the [[White House]] and [[United States Department of Justice|Justice Department]].<ref>{{Cite news|last=Anas|first=Brittany|work=Daily Camera|url=http://www.dailycamera.com/archivesearch/ci_13118756|title=CU law professor appointed to Justice Department's antitrust division|date=April 22, 2009|access-date=June 14, 2017}}</ref><ref>{{cite journal|title=Federal Communications Commission Process Reform Act of 2012|journal=Congressional Record, 112th Congress, 2nd Session|date=March 27, 2012|volume=158|issue=50|page=H1610|url=https://www.congress.gov/congressional-record/2012/3/27/house-section/article/h1609-1|access-date=September 16, 2017|quote=Former White House adviser Philip Weiser}}</ref> A member of the Democratic Party, he was elected [[Colorado Attorney General|Attorney General for the State of Colorado]] in the [[2018 Colorado Attorney General election|2018 election]], defeating [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] George Brauchler on November 6, 2018.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web|url=https://www.cpr.org/news/story/salazar-concedes-phil-weiser-to-face-george-brauchler-for-ag-in-november|title=Salazar Concedes; Phil Weiser To Face George Brauchler For Attorney General In November|work=Colorado Public Radio|date= July 2, 2018|access-date=July 4, 2018}}</ref> He was re-elected in [[2022 Colorado Attorney General election|2022]].<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |last=Sherry |first=Allison |date=November 8, 2022 |title=Phil Weiser wins a second term as Colorado attorney general |url=https://www.cpr.org/2022/11/08/colorado-attorney-general-race-results-phil-weiser-john-kellner-2022-election/ |access-date=November 25, 2022 |website=[[Colorado Public Radio]] |language=en}}</ref>

On January 2, 2025, Weiser announced his candidacy in the [[2026 Colorado gubernatorial election]].<ref name="Paul2025">{{cite web |last1=Paul |first1=Jesse |title=Colorado Attorney General Phil Weiser launches 2026 campaign for governor |url=https://coloradosun.com/2025/01/02/phil-weiser-colorado-governor-2026/ |website=Colorado Sun |access-date=January 2, 2025 |date=January 2, 2025}}</ref> He won the Democratic nomination on June 30, 2026, defeating U.S. senator [[Michael Bennet]] in the primary.

== Early life and education ==
Weiser was born to an [[Ashkenazi Jews|Ashkenazi Jewish]] family. His grandparents survived the [[Holocaust]], and his mother, Estare, was born in the [[Buchenwald concentration camp]] in 1945.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Luning |first1=Ernest |title=Holocaust survivor Estare Weiser featured in ad for her son Phil Weiser's attorney general campaign |url=https://www.coloradopolitics.com/hot-sheet/holocaust-survivor-estare-weiser-featured-in-ad-for-her-son-phil-weisers-attorney-general-campaign/article_2f52ff72-e9d9-52ff-b44d-8c941f609fed.html |website=Colorado Politics |access-date=March 4, 2021 |language=en |date=February 22, 2018}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last1=Young|first1=Lisa|title=Attorney General candidate Phil Weiser stumps in Sterling|url=http://www.journal-advocate.com/sterling-local_news/ci_31164636/attorney-general-candidate-phil-weiser-stumps-sterling|access-date=September 14, 2017|work=Journal-Advocate (Sterling, CO)|date=July 24, 2017}}</ref>

After high school, Weiser studied [[political science]] at [[Swarthmore College]], graduating in 1990 with a [[Bachelor of Arts]] with high honors. He then attended the [[New York University School of Law]], where he was an Articles Editor for the ''[[New York University Law Review]]''. He graduated from NYU Law in 1994 with a [[Juris Doctor]] degree and [[Order of the Coif]] honors.<ref>{{cite web|title=Philip Weiser '94 named dean of the University of Colorado Law School|url=http://www.law.nyu.edu/news/weiser_dean_colorado_law|publisher=NYU Law School News|access-date=September 13, 2017|date=May 31, 2011}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=Masthead for 1993-94, Vol 68-69|url=http://www.nyulawreview.org/about/mastheads/1993-94-vol-68-69|publisher=New York University Law Review|access-date=September 15, 2017|date=January 2, 2025}}</ref>

== Academic and federal government career ==

=== Law clerk and Clinton administration===
After graduating, Weiser served as law clerk to [[David M. Ebel|Judge David Ebel]] of the [[United States Court of Appeals for the Tenth Circuit|Tenth Circuit Court of Appeals]] from September 1994 to August 1995. He was then a law clerk to [[Byron White|Justices Byron R. White]] and [[Ruth Bader Ginsburg]] in the [[Supreme Court of the United States|U.S. Supreme Court]] from September 1995 to August 1996. Following his clerkships, he was senior counsel to [[Joel Klein]], the [[United States Assistant Attorney General|Assistant Attorney General]] for the Justice Department's [[United States Department of Justice Antitrust Division|Antitrust Division]] from 1996 to 1998.<ref name="faculty" />

=== Academic career ===
In 1999, Weiser joined the [[University of Colorado Boulder|University of Colorado]] [[University of Colorado Law School|Law School]] in [[Boulder, Colorado|Boulder]] as a professor of law and telecommunications. There, Weiser established the national center of excellence in telecommunications and technology law and founded the ''Journal on Telecommunications & High Technology Law''.<ref name="faculty" /><ref>[http://www.jthtl.org/ ''Journal on Telecommunications and High Technology Law'']. jthtl.org.</ref> He also founded the [https://www.colorado.edu/law/research/siliconflatirons Silicon Flatirons Center for Law, Technology, and Entrepreneurship] and he wrote and taught in the areas of competition policy, innovation policy, and Internet policy.<ref name="Kata2017">{{Cite news|last=Kata|first=Sarah|work=Daily Camera|url=http://www.dailycamera.com/cu-news/ci_30738872/phil-weiser-lead-new-innovation-entrepreneurship-initiative-at|title=Phil Weiser to lead new innovation, entrepreneurship initiative at CU Boulder|date=January 20, 2017|access-date=June 14, 2017}}</ref><ref name="faculty" /><ref>{{cite web|last1=Weiser|first1=Phil|title=Boulder is for Startups|url=https://obamawhitehouse.archives.gov/blog/2011/05/11/boulder-startups|publisher=Obama Whitehouse Archive|access-date=September 15, 2017|date=May 11, 2011|quote=having worked at the University of Colorado Law School and run the Silicon Flatirons Center for Law, Technology, and Entrepreneurship from 1999 until joining the Obama Administration in 2009}}</ref>

From June 2011 through July 2016, Weiser served as the fifteenth dean of the law school,<ref name="Anas2011">{{cite news|last1=Anas|first1=Brittany|title=CU-Boulder names Phil Weiser dean of law school|url=http://www.dailycamera.com/ci_18174823|access-date=September 13, 2017|work=Daily Camera|date=May 31, 2011}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last1=Mendoza|first1=Monica|title=CU Law School Dean Weiser stepping down|url=https://www.bizjournals.com/denver/blog/finance_etc/2015/08/cu-law-school-dean-weiser-stepping-down-going-back.html?ana=e_du_pap&s=article_du&ed=2015-08-24&u=rFuEtNnpCoSO1qd+FguvKNdYeb8&t=1440451907|access-date=September 16, 2017|work=Denver Business Journal|date=August 24, 2015|quote=He also organized the Gathering of the Bench and Bar Conference, worked with the White Center to establish the Stevens Lecture, which brought four Supreme Court Justices to Colorado Law}}</ref> and he was named one of the National Jurist's most influential leaders in legal education.<ref>{{Cite web|work=The National Jurist|url=http://www.nationaljurist.com/prelaw/25-finalists-named-most-influential-legal-education|title=25 Finalists Named to Most Influential in Legal Education|date=November 28, 2012 |access-date=June 13, 2017}}</ref> Through the Silicon Flatirons Center, Weiser developed a range of programs to build up CU Boulder's support for entrepreneurship and has linked it to the local startup community.<ref>{{Cite news|url=http://www.colorado.edu/today/2017/01/19/weiser-lead-innovation-and-entrepreneurship-initiative|title=Weiser to lead Innovation and Entrepreneurship initiative|date=January 19, 2017|work=CU Boulder Today|access-date=June 13, 2017|language=en}}</ref><ref name="Kata2017"/> Some of the initiatives include Tech Lawyer Accelerator, the Corporate Counsel Intensive Institute and the [[Daniels Fund]] Ethics Initiative.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.politico.com/story/2016/06/inside-clintons-tech-policy-circle-223992|title=Inside Clinton's tech policy circle|work=POLITICO|access-date=June 13, 2017}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|url=http://www.colorado.edu/today/2015/08/24/philip-weiser-step-down-dean-university-colorado-law-school|title=Philip Weiser to step down as dean of the University of Colorado Law School|date=August 24, 2015|work=CU Boulder Today|access-date=June 13, 2017|language=en}}</ref>

=== Obama administration ===
[[File:Weiser Family with President Obama.jpg|thumb|Weiser and his family with President Obama in the [[Oval Office]]]]
In 2009, President [[Barack Obama]] appointed Weiser as [[Deputy Assistant Attorney-General|Deputy Assistant Attorney General]] in the Justice Department's [[United States Department of Justice Antitrust Division|Antitrust Division]].<ref>{{cite web|last1=Chopra|first1=Aneesh|title=Providing Leadership on Standards to Address National Challenges|url=https://obamawhitehouse.archives.gov/blog/2010/03/24/providing-leadership-standards-address-national-challenges|publisher=Obama White House Archives|access-date=September 15, 2017|date=March 24, 2010|quote=Philip Weiser, Deputy Assistant Attorney General for International, Policy and Appellate Matters, Department of Justice}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|url=http://lawweb.colorado.edu/news/showArticle.jsp?id=517|title=Press release: Professor Weiser Appointed to Justice Department's Antitrust Division|publisher=University of Colorado Law School|date=April 22, 2009|access-date=June 14, 2017}}</ref> He took the post in July 2009, taking a leave of absence from the [[University of Colorado Law School]].<ref name="Anas2011"/> In 2010, President Obama named him senior advisor for technology and innovation to the [[National Economic Council (United States)#Directors of the National Economic Council|National Economic Council Director]], and he participated in a series of policy initiatives.<ref>{{cite web|title=American Association for the Advancement of Science Agenda|url=https://obamawhitehouse.archives.gov/sites/default/files/microsites/ostp/nitrd-event-agenda.pdf|publisher=Obama White House Archives|access-date=September 15, 2017|date=December 16, 2010|quote=Phil Weiser, Senior Advisor for Technology and Innovation to the National Economic Council Director}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|last1=Chopra|first1=Aneesh|title=Innovation for America: Technology for Economic Growth and Empowering Americans|url=https://obamawhitehouse.archives.gov/blog/2010/06/15/innovation-america-technology-economic-growth-and-empowering-americans|publisher=Obama White House Archives|access-date=September 15, 2017|date=June 15, 2010|quote=Phil Weiser, Senior Advisor for Technology and Innovation to the National Economic Council Director}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|last=Moore|first=Tom|work=TimesArrow|url=http://www.timesarrow.net/blog/b0lhn0r2plxlexqaszpkh41c8wdvrf|title=Phil Weiser appointed as White House Senior Advisor for Technology and Innovation|date=April 29, 2010|access-date=June 14, 2017}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|url=https://obamawhitehouse.archives.gov/the-press-office/2011/02/10/president-obama-details-plan-win-future-through-expanded-wireless-access|title=President Obama Details Plan to Win the Future through Expanded Wireless Access|date=February 10, 2011|website=Obama White House Archives|access-date=June 14, 2017}}</ref>

