Weiser was elected [[Colorado Attorney General|Attorney General for the State of Colorado]] in the [[2018 Colorado Attorney General election|2018 election]], defeating Republican George Brauchler on November 6, 2018.<ref name=":0" /> Weiser took office in January 2019, becoming the first Democratic Colorado Attorney General in 15 years. Later that same month, Weiser withdrew Colorado from a lawsuit that his predecessor, Republican Cynthia Coffman, had filed against the [[Clean Power Plan]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://apnews.com/9d735890bc9f443891aa0f3c6314bc44|title=With Democrats in charge, Colorado now backs clean air rule|last=Elliott|first=Dan|date=January 30, 2019|website=AP NEWS|access-date=January 31, 2019}}</ref>

In the [[2022 Colorado Attorney General election]], Weiser was re-elected for a second term, defeating Republican challenger John Kellner and winning over 54% of votes cast.<ref name=":1" />

=== Tenure ===

====Lawsuits against the Trump administration====
Since President Donald Trump took office in January 2025, Weiser has filed or joined more than 65 lawsuits challenging Trump administration policies. Weiser's lawsuits range in topics, but have been mostly focused on funding cuts, the environment, immigration, and federal firings. As of June 2026, the ''[[The Colorado Sun|Colorado Sun]]''{{'s}} continuously updated tracker reported that courts had partially or fully blocked the challenged policies in 36 cases, left them in place in 8 cases, and were still considering 22.<ref name="coloradosun-tracker">{{cite news |last=Dolven |first=Taylor |orig-date=August 4, 2025 |date=June 17, 2026 |title=Colorado attorney general has sued Trump 66 times since inauguration. So far, he's mostly won. |url=https://coloradosun.com/2025/08/04/colorado-attorney-general-phil-weiser-lawsuits-trump/ |work=The Colorado Sun |access-date=June 18, 2026}}</ref>  At a February 2026 news conference, Weiser said that his federal litigation had restored or protected more than $1 billion in Colorado funding.<ref name="coloradosun-clawback">{{cite news |last=Booth |first=Michael |date=February 19, 2026 |title=Colorado AG sues to claw back $600 million in energy research grants canceled by Trump |url=https://coloradosun.com/2026/02/19/colorado-clawback-trump-energy-grants-lawsuits/ |work=The Colorado Sun |access-date=May 18, 2026}}</ref> The following cases, selected for their impact on Colorado and presented in chronological order, illustrate the range of Weiser's federal litigation.

On April 1, 2025, Weiser's office led a coalition of 25 states and territories in suing the [[U.S. Department of Health and Human Services]] over its abrupt termination of $11 billion in federal public health funding the previous week. The administration had justified the termination by declaring the [[COVID-19 pandemic in the United States|COVID-19 pandemic]] over and the grants therefore "no longer necessary," although Congress had reauthorized the funding as recently as June 2023, after the formal end of the pandemic emergency. The complaint states that the affected funds supported infectious-disease tracking, immunization access, emergency preparedness, mental health and substance abuse services, and public health data infrastructure across the plaintiff states. In Colorado specifically, the terminations threatened the state's Behavioral Health Administration and 68 of its grantee partners, including the Mobile Crisis Response program and the Assertive Community Treatment program, which served more than 650 individuals with serious mental illness.<ref name="cocomplaint-hhs-2025">{{cite court |litigants=State of Colorado et al. v. U.S. Department of Health and Human Services et al. |court=D.R.I. |date=April 1, 2025 |url=https://ag.ny.gov/sites/default/files/court-filings/colorado-et-al-v-us-department-of-health-and-human-services-et-al-complaint-2025.pdf}}</ref> The court granted a temporary restraining order within two days of filing, entered a preliminary injunction six weeks later requiring HHS to restore the funding, and saw the administration's appeal voluntarily dismissed shortly thereafter.<ref name="clearinghouse-cohhs">{{cite web |title=State of Colorado v. U.S. Department of Health and Human Services |url=https://clearinghouse.net/case/46327/ |website=Civil Rights Litigation Clearinghouse |publisher=University of Michigan Law School |access-date=May 18, 2026}}</ref> 

On October 28, 2025, Weiser joined attorneys general from 25 states in a lawsuit to prevent the Trump administration from suspending payments under the [[Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program]] (SNAP).<ref name="cbs-snap-suit">{{cite news |last=Quinn |first=Melissa |date=October 31, 2025 |title=25 states sue Trump administration over SNAP food stamp freeze during shutdown |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/news/snap-food-stamps-lawsuit-25-states-trump-administration/ |work=CBS News |access-date=June 18, 2026}}</ref><ref name="courtlistener-mass-v-usda">{{cite court |litigants=Commonwealth of Massachusetts v. United States Department of Agriculture, No. 1:25-cv-13165 |court=D. Mass. |date=October 28, 2025 |url=https://www.courtlistener.com/docket/71783393/commonwealth-of-massachusetts-v-united-states-department-of-agriculture/}}</ref> SNAP provides approximately $120 million in benefits to 600,000 Coloradans each month.<ref name="conewsline-snap">{{cite news |last=Wilson |first=Sara |date=October 28, 2025 |title=Colorado AG Weiser joins lawsuit against federal government over SNAP benefit pause |url=https://coloradonewsline.com/briefs/weiser-joins-lawsuit-snap-benefit/ |work=Colorado Newsline |access-date=June 18, 2026}}</ref> [[United States Department of Agriculture|USDA]] had taken the position that it lacked authority to use approximately $5 billion in SNAP contingency funds to cover November 2025 benefits during the ongoing federal government shutdown.<ref name="cbs-snap-suit" /> On May 1, 2026, USDA reversed course and agreed not to withhold SNAP benefits. The parties then jointly asked the court to dismiss the case, which it did.<ref name="courtlistener-mass-v-usda-126">{{cite court |litigants=Commonwealth of Massachusetts v. United States Department of Agriculture, No. 1:25-cv-13165 |court=D. Mass. |date=May 1, 2026 |opinion=Order Dismissing Case |url=https://www.courtlistener.com/docket/71783393/126/commonwealth-of-massachusetts-v-united-states-department-of-agriculture/}}</ref>

On January 9, 2026, Weiser joined attorneys general from California, Illinois, Minnesota, and New York in suing the Trump administration over a freeze of more than $10 billion in federal funding for child care, cash assistance, and social services programs across the five states. The administration had announced the freeze days earlier, citing concerns about fraud and benefits going to undocumented immigrants, although undocumented immigrants do not qualify for the [[Temporary Assistance for Needy Families]] (TANF) program or Colorado's child care assistance program under existing rules. The same day the suit was filed, the court granted a temporary restraining order requiring the administration to release the funds.<ref name="coloradosun-tanf-freeze-2026">{{cite news |last=Dolven |first=Taylor |date=January 9, 2026 |title=Judge halts Trump administration funding cuts for Colorado's poorest families after lawsuit from Weiser, other attorneys general |url=https://coloradosun.com/2026/01/09/phil-weiser-sues-trump-child-care-funding/ |work=The Colorado Sun |access-date=May 18, 2026}}</ref> A preliminary injunction followed in February 2026, blocking the administration from re-imposing the restrictions. The three programs at issue provide Colorado with approximately $300 million annually and support more than 27,000 children in the state's child care assistance program, more than 50,000 children through TANF-funded cash assistance, and another 14,400 children through prevention and intervention services.<ref name="pagosasun-injunction-2026">{{cite news |date=February 12, 2026 |title=Judge grants injunction on funding freeze for child care, support programs |url=https://www.pagosasun.com/stories/judge-grants-injunction-on-funding-freeze-for-child-care-support-programs,124795 |work=The Pagosa Springs Sun |access-date=May 18, 2026}}</ref>

On February 18, 2026, Weiser's office co-led a coalition of 13 state attorneys general, with California Attorney General [[Rob Bonta]] and Washington Attorney General [[Nicholas W. Brown (lawyer)|Nick Brown]], in filing suit opposing the Trump administration's termination of approximately $8 billion in clean-energy and infrastructure awards nationwide, including more than $600 million for Colorado projects affecting [[Colorado State University]], the [[Colorado School of Mines]], and the [[University of Colorado]]. The complaint alleged that the executive branch could not unilaterally cancel funding Congress had appropriated under the 2021 [[Bipartisan Infrastructure Law]] and the 2022 [[Inflation Reduction Act]]. The case remained pending as of mid-2026.<ref name="coloradosun-clawback" /><ref name="cpr-cleanenergy-2026">{{cite news |last=Thakore |first=Ishan |date=February 19, 2026 |title=Colorado joins group of Democratic states suing Trump administration over clean energy cuts |url=https://www.cpr.org/2026/02/19/clean-energy-colorado-lawsuit-weiser-trump-administration-methane-oil-and-gas/ |work=Colorado Public Radio |access-date=May 18, 2026}}</ref><ref name="scd-doe-lawsuit-2026">{{cite news |last=Lawrence |first=Robyn Griggs |date=February 20, 2026 |title=States sue Energy Department for terminating $8B in clean energy funding |url=https://www.smartcitiesdive.com/news/states-sue-doe-terminating-8b-clean-energy-funding/812670/ |work=Smart Cities Dive |access-date=May 18, 2026}}</ref>

====Opioid settlement====
In July 2019, Weiser amended Colorado's existing lawsuit against [[Purdue Pharma]] to name eight members of the [[Sackler family]], which privately owned Purdue, along with several former company executives. The amended complaint alleges "fraudulent and reckless conduct" under three Colorado statutes: the Consumer Protection Act, the Organized Crime Control Act, and the Uniform Fraudulent Transfer Act. The complaint also alleges that warnings about addiction risk were brushed aside in pursuit of sales.<ref name="cpr-2019-purdue">{{cite news|url=https://www.cpr.org/2019/07/01/colorado-expands-lawsuit-against-maker-of-oxycontin/|title=Colorado Expands Lawsuit Against Maker Of OxyContin|first=Natalia|last=Navarro|website=Colorado Public Radio|date=July 1, 2019|access-date=May 7, 2026}}</ref>

In total, the lawsuits Weiser filed and those he joined have brought more than $912 million to Colorado, including at least $32 million from an April 2026 settlement with the [[Albertsons]] grocery chain.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.denver7.com/news/local-news/albertsons-grocery-chain-to-pay-more-than-773-million-in-planned-opioid-settlements/ |title= Albertsons grocery chain to pay more than $773 million in planned opioid settlements |first=Kaylee|last=Harter|website= Denver 7 ABC|date=April 14, 2026|access-date=May 7, 2026 }}</ref>

====Tenant protections====
Weiser has made housing a major focus of his tenure, pursuing legal action against large rental property owners and managers accused of unfair trade practices.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.denverpost.com/2025/11/19/colorado-greystar-rent-realpage/ |title=Colorado reaches settlement with landlord giant Greystar in price-fixing lawsuit |first=Seth |last=Klamann |website=The Denver Post |date=November 19, 2025|access-date=May 8, 2026 }}</ref>

In a joint action with the [[Federal Trade Commission]], Weiser's office alleged what it called a "pattern of deceptive pricing" identified through a [[Consumer Financial Protection Bureau|Consumer Protection Bureau]] investigation. According to the state, [[Greystar]] advertised attractive monthly rents while concealing additional mandatory recurring charges covering services such as pest control, trash removal, concierge services, utility management, and amenity access. The state alleged that prospective tenants could not discover the full cost of a unit until after signing a lease and paying non-refundable application fees.<ref name="dev-gaz-greystar">{{cite news|url=https://www.denvergazette.com/2025/12/03/apartment-giant-greystar-settles-with-colorado-over-fraudulent-advertising/ |title=Apartment giant Greystar settles with Colorado over fraudulent advertising |first=Mark |last=Samuelson |website=The Denver Gazette |date=December 3, 2025|access-date=May 8, 2026 }}</ref>

Greystar settled with the state of Colorado for more than $1 million. According to the state's announcement, the funds would be used for "reimbursement of actual costs and attorneys' fees, future consumer protection or antitrust enforcement, consumer education, or public welfare purposes."<ref name="dev-gaz-greystar" /> Under the terms, Greystar must disclose the full monthly cost of leasing a unit, broken down by fee category, and may no longer require deposits before complete pricing has been disclosed to the tenant.<ref name="dev-gaz-greystar" />

In a separate matter, Colorado joined the [[United States Department of Justice|U.S. Department of Justice]] and seven other states in an [[antitrust]] suit against [[RealPage]], the Texas-based company that dominates the market for landlord revenue-management software.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://coloradosun.com/2024/08/23/realpage-lawsuit-colorado-price-fixing/ |title=Colorado, U.S. Department of Justice sue RealPage over alleged price-fixing scheme to drive up rent |first=Brian |last=Eason |website=The Colorado Sun|date=August 23, 2024|access-date=May 8, 2026 }}</ref> Weiser's office later amended the complaint to add Greystar, [[LivCor]], [[Camden Property Trust]], [[Cushman & Wakefield]], Willow Bridge, and Cortland as defendants, alleging the companies coordinated through RealPage's software to suppress competition on rental pricing. Greystar alone operates more than 45,000 rental units in Colorado.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.denverpost.com/2025/01/07/colorado-justice-department-realpage-lawsuit-landlords-rent-increases-greystar-cortland/|title=Colorado, Justice Department add Greystar, Cortland and other large landlords to apartment price-fixing suit |first=Seth |last=Klamann |website=The Denver Post|date=January 7, 2025|access-date=May 8, 2026 }}</ref>

Weiser also reached a settlement with Baron Property Services, which the state alleged had charged tenants for the company's master renters-insurance policy even when those tenants already carried qualifying third-party coverage, in violation of the Colorado Consumer Protection Act. The state separately alleged that Baron, when screening rental applicants, had treated unadjudicated criminal charges as convictions, violating the Rental Application Fairness Act. The settlement required Baron to pay approximately $7,300 in restitution to the 368 affected tenants and an additional $67,635 to the state, and to bring its policies into compliance with both statutes.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.montrosepress.com/news/records/weiser-announces-settlement-with-baron-property-services-wins-payments-for-renters/article_d3b21765-7bb6-4096-a9e8-77065d54c857.html |title=Weiser announces settlement with Baron Property Services, wins payments for renters |website=Montrose Press |date=April 13, 2026|access-date=May 8, 2026 }}</ref>

====PFAS contamination====
In February 2022, Weiser filed a lawsuit against 15 companies that manufactured a firefighting foam containing chemicals known as [[PFAS]] (or "forever chemicals"), seeking to make them pay for investigation, cleanup, and monitoring of contamination sites across Colorado. PFAS "have been tied to cancer, birth defects and other illnesses."<ref name="denver7-weiser-afff-2022">{{cite news |last=Miller |first=Blair |date=February 28, 2022 |title=Colorado AG sues makers of firefighting foam with 'forever chemicals' |url=https://www.denver7.com/news/local-news/colorado-ag-sues-makers-of-firefighting-foam-with-forever-chemicals |work=Denver7 |publisher=KMGH-TV |access-date=May 17, 2026}}</ref>

According to the complaint, PFAS have been found in soil, surface and ground water in 50 of Colorado's 64 counties. In a 2020 sample covering half of Colorado's public drinking water systems, 34% had detectable PFAS. Contamination sites identified in the complaint spanned the Front Range and included Peterson Air Force Base and Fort Carson near Colorado Springs, Buckley Space Force Base in the Denver area, the U.S. Air Force Academy, the Suncor plant, Colorado's federal airports, and fire districts in Teller and Boulder counties.<ref name="denver7-weiser-afff-2022" />

In July 2023, Weiser was among 22 state and territorial attorneys general who filed an opposition brief asking the court to reject a proposed $10.3 billion class-action settlement between [[3M]] and the nation's public water providers. The coalition argued that the settlement's structure was inadequate. Every public water provider in the country would be swept into the settlement automatically and would have to take affirmative steps to escape it, even providers that had never sued 3M or tested their water for PFAS.<ref name="ap-22ags-3m-2023">{{cite news |agency=Associated Press |date=July 27, 2023 |title=22 attorneys general oppose 3M settlement over water systems contamination with 'forever chemicals' |url=https://www.mprnews.org/story/2023/07/26/22-attorneys-general-oppose-3m-settlement-over-water-systems-contamination |work=MPR News |access-date=May 17, 2026}}</ref>

Providers facing that opt-out decision would not yet know how much money they stood to receive under the settlement, nor in many cases would they have determined whether their systems were contaminated or what remediation might cost. The coalition also objected to an indemnification provision under which 3M could later seek to recover from settling water providers any damages the company paid to third parties.<ref name="legalnewsline-2023">{{cite news |last=O'Brien |first=John |date=July 27, 2023 |title=States want changes to $12.5B PFAS settlement with 3M |url=https://www.legalnewsline.com/states-want-changes-to-12-5b-pfas-settlement-with-3m/article_ce5be1f6-8294-5ebe-8190-363e1723965b.html |work=Legal Newsline |access-date=May 17, 2026}}</ref> For example, if residents of a contaminated community developed cancer and sued 3M, the company could turn around and demand reimbursement from the local water utility.<ref name="ap-22ags-3m-2023" />

On March 29, 2024, the court granted final approval to the settlement with the public water providers, with revisions that raised the payment range to $10.5 to $12.5 billion over 13 years. Of roughly 12,000 covered public water systems nationwide, about 897 opted out. Payments under the agreement began in the third quarter of 2024.<ref name="court-final-approval-2024">{{cite web |title=Order Granting Final Approval, In re Aqueous Film-Forming Foams Products Liability Litigation, MDL 2873 |date=March 29, 2024 |publisher=U.S. District Court for the District of South Carolina |url=https://www.pfaswatersettlement.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/3M-Final-Approval-Order.pdf |access-date=May 17, 2026}}</ref><ref name="manufacturing-dive-2024">{{cite news |last=Magill |first=Kate |date=April 4, 2024 |title=3M PFAS settlement gets final approval |url=https://www.manufacturingdive.com/news/3m-pfas-water-supply-contamination-settlement-final-approval-forever-chemicals/712209/ |work=Manufacturing Dive |access-date=May 17, 2026}}</ref> The settlement covered only claims by public water suppliers; it did not include the separate, ongoing PFAS-related lawsuits that states, including Colorado, had filed against manufacturers.<ref name="court-final-approval-2024" />

====Catholic Church investigation====
On October 23, 2019, Weiser released the results of an eight-month investigation revealing that 43 [[Catholic Church|Catholic]] clergy were credibly accused of sexually abusing at least 166 children throughout the state of Colorado since 1950.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.denverpost.com/2019/10/23/colorado-catholic-church-sex-abuse-report/|title= Investigator finds 43 Catholic priests in Colorado sexually abused at least 166 children |first=Elise|last=Schmelzer|website= The Denver Post|date=October 24, 2019 }}</ref> On October 16, 2020, it was revealed that all three of Colorado's Catholic Dioceses, the [[Archdiocese of Denver]], the [[Diocese of Colorado Springs]], and [[Diocese of Pueblo]], had paid $6.6 million in compensation to 81 victims of clergy sex abuse within the past year, regardless of how long ago the abuse happened.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://denver.cbslocal.com/2020/10/16/catholic-dioceses-colorado-pay-6-6-million-sex-abuse-victims/|title=Catholic Dioceses In Colorado Pay $6.6 Million To Sex Abuse Survivors|first=Anica|last=Padilla|publisher=[[KCNC-TV|CBS 4 Denver]]|date=October 16, 2020|access-date=December 1, 2020}}</ref>

On December 1, 2020, Weiser's final report revealed that there were an additional 9 credibly accused clergy and 46 alleged victims in both in the Archdiocese of Denver and its suffragan Diocese of Pueblo.<ref name="homelessshelterman">{{cite news|url=https://www.denverpost.com/2020/12/01/colorado-catholic-church-priest-abuse-report/|title=Further investigation into Colorado Catholic Church IDs 46 more victims, 9 more abusive priests — including Denver's Father Woody|first=Elise|last=Schmelzer|publisher=Denver Post|date=December 1, 2020|access-date=December 1, 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.cpr.org/2020/12/01/final-state-report-concludes-more-than-200-colorado-children-were-abused-by-priests-but-catholic-church-vows-reform/|title=Final State Report Concludes More Than 200 Colorado Children Were Abused By Priests, Catholic Church Vows Reform|first=Allison|last=Sherry|publisher=CPR News|date=December 1, 2020|access-date=December 1, 2020}}</ref> Statewide, 52 Colorado Catholic priests were named in Weiser's final report as committing acts of sex abuse.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.ucanews.com/news/colorado-report-names-nine-more-priests-accused-of-abusing-minors-decades-ago/90582|title=Colorado report names nine more priests accused of abusing minors decades ago|author=Julie Asher, Catholic News Service|publisher=Union of Catholic Asian News|date=December 7, 2020|access-date=December 7, 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://coloradosun.com/2020/12/01/colorado-catholic-church-priest-abuse-father-woody/|title=52 Catholic priests in Colorado, including iconic Father Woody, abused 212 victims, further investigation finds|first1=Jesse|last1=Paul|first2=Jennifer|last2=Brown|publisher=The Colorado Sun|date=December 1, 2020|access-date=December 7, 2020}}</ref> Prominent Archdiocese of Denver priest Fr. Charles B. Woodrich, also known as "Father Woody," was among those listed.<ref name="homelessshelterman" /><ref name="fatherwoody">{{cite news|url=https://denver.cbslocal.com/2020/12/01/father-woody-charles-woodrich-priests-named-colorado-child-sex-abuse-report/|title=Father Woody Among 9 Additional Priests Named In New Colorado Child Sex Abuse Report|first=Anica|last=Padilla|publisher=[[KCNC-TV|CBS 4 Denver]]|date=December 1, 2020|access-date=December 1, 2020}}</ref> Father Woody was known for his work in local homeless shelters.<ref name="homelessshelterman" /><ref name="fatherwoody" />

====Economic competition====
Affordability has been another area Weiser has concentrated on heavily. On February 14, 2024, he filed a lawsuit in Denver District Court over $24.6 billion [[Attempted acquisition of Albertsons by Kroger|Kroger-Albertsons merger]], as he said this would lead to "stores closing, higher prices, fewer jobs, worse customer service, and less resilient supply chains.”<ref>{{cite web |last=Paul |first=Jesse |date=September 30, 2024 |title=Colorado trial over Kroger-Albertsons merger lawsuit is underway |url=https://www.chieftain.com/story/news/2024/09/30/colorado-trial-over-kroger-albertsons-merger-suit-underway/75452290007/ |website=Pueblo Chieftan |access-date=April 23, 2026 |language=en-us}}</ref> On December 10, 2024, the merger was blocked by a federal district court.

== 2026 Colorado gubernatorial campaign ==
{{main|2026 Colorado gubernatorial election}}
On January 2, 2025, Weiser announced his candidacy to replace [[Term limit|term-limited]] [[Colorado Governor]] [[Jared Polis]] in the [[2026 Colorado gubernatorial election]].<ref name="Paul2025"/> Weiser's campaign began 2026 with approximately $3.5 million and raised $822,000 in the first quarter of 2026. Significant donors included [[Merle Chambers]] and [[Pat Meyers]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Paul |first1=Jesse |title=Michael Bennet, Phil Weiser and their supporters amass combined $9M for high-stakes gubernatorial primary |url=https://coloradosun.com/2026/01/16/michael-bennet-phil-weiser-campaign-cash/ |work=The Colorado Sun |date=January 16, 2026}}</ref> Weiser's campaign savings surpassed Bennet's to start 2026, but Rocky Mountain Way, a [[Super PAC]] supporting Bennet, made the total funds for each candidate similar.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Luning |first1=Ernest |title=Democrats Michael Bennet, Phil Weiser and their backers bank $9 million for Colorado gubernatorial race |url=https://www.coloradopolitics.com/2026/01/20/democrats-michael-bennet-phil-weiser-and-their-backers-bank-9-million-for-colorado-gubernatorial-race/ |work=Colorado Politics |date=January 20, 2026}}</ref>

At the [[Colorado Democratic Party]]'s State Assembly on March 28, 2026, Weiser won 90% of the delegate vote, and appeared at the top of the ballot for the Colorado Democratic primary on June 30th.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Woodruff |first=Chase |date=March 29, 2026 |title=Weiser, Gonzales take top spots on Colorado’s 2026 Democratic primary ballot |url=https://coloradonewsline.com/2026/03/29/weiser-gonzales-2026-democratic-assembly/ |access-date=April 13, 2026 |website=Colorado Newsline |language=en-US}}</ref>

On June 30, 2026, Weiser won the [[2026 Colorado gubernatorial election|Colorado Democratic gubernatorial primary]] with 56.7% of the vote, beating U.S. Senator [[Michael Bennet]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=July 2, 2026 |title=Colorado Governor Primary Election 2026 Live Results: Bennet, Weiser and More |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2026-primary-elections/colorado-governor-results |access-date=July 2, 2026 |website=www.nbcnews.com |language=en}}</ref>

== Personal life ==
In 2002, Weiser married Heidi Wald, a physician, in [[Philadelphia]], Pennsylvania, and they now live together with their two children in [[Denver]].<ref name="denverpost">{{Cite web|url=http://www.denverpost.com/2017/05/11/phil-weiser-attorney-general-colorado/|last=Frank|first=John|title=Phil Weiser announces bid for Colorado attorney general with big-time endorsement|work=The Denver Post|date= May 11, 2017|access-date=June 13, 2017}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=Weddings/Celebrations: Heidi Wald, Philip Weiser|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2002/11/10/style/weddings-celebrations-heidi-wald-philip-weiser.html|access-date=September 14, 2017|work=New York Times|date=November 10, 2002}}</ref>

